  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 17,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.517,
            0.042,
            0.848,
            0.059
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Exploring Mixtures and their Separation"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.856,
            0.018,
            0.952,
            0.088
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "N 89"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.088,
            0.774,
            0.156
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We use various properties, such as size, density and solubility to separate mixtures. However, separation is not always simple. Imagine trying to separate all the ingredients in a lemonade once they have been mixed. Can you do it?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.159,
            0.775,
            0.279
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Do you know that separation happens in nature and in our bodies, too? For example, our kidneys remove waste from our blood. Today, we face bigger challenges like cleaning oceans and rivers by removing plastic waste. It is also essential to treat sewage water before releasing it into the environment. Sewage treatment involves steps, such as sedimentation, coagulation and filtration. Clean water obtained in this way can be reused for flushing toilets or watering plants."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.281,
            0.775,
            0.367
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Another important task is sorting or segregating the waste at home. Dry waste, such as plastic, paper, glass and metal can be recycled, while wet waste like food scraps, vegetable peels, etc., can be composted. Researchers are also finding ways to recover valuable materials, such as lithium from old batteries used in mobile phones and laptops."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.368,
            0.773,
            0.404
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "By improving the methods for separating waste and recycling materials, you can help make the world cleaner, healthier and more sustainable."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.14,
            0.412,
            0.29,
            0.432
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "At a Glance"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.13,
            0.445,
            0.755,
            0.48
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Mixtures are classified as homogeneous or heterogeneous and also as solutions, suspensions, or colloids."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.481,
            0.755,
            0.514
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Separation of mixtures is a crucial process for obtaining pure substances."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.515,
            0.755,
            0.55
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Crystallization is a technique used to get a pure solid from its saturated solution."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.55,
            0.756,
            0.601
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Distillation is used to separate two miscible liquids with a minimum difference of 25 °C in boiling points. It can also be used to obtain a liquid from a mixture of a liquid and a solid."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.602,
            0.756,
            0.653
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Paper chromatography can be used to separate compounds by taking advantage of differences in their rates of movement on the paper."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.654,
            0.755,
            0.688
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "A separating funnel is used to separate two immiscible liquids based on their different densities."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.689,
            0.756,
            0.756
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The transition of a solid directly into a vapour (below its melting point) without passing through the liquid state is called sublimation. The process when the vapour cools and changes back into a solid without becoming a liquid is called deposition."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.757,
            0.755,
            0.792
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Centrifugation uses rapid spinning to separate heavier solid particles from a solid-liquid mixture."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.792,
            0.755,
            0.843
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Coagulation involves the addition of a substance, called a coagulant, to make smaller particles clump together and settle down in a heterogeneous mixture of solids and liquids."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.843,
            0.755,
            0.878
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The dispersed particles in a colloid or suspension scatter light, which is known as the Tyndall effect."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
